Exactly. The amount communicated isn’t the same as the amount said.

A thousand words can conceal an absence of meaning. A glance can convey an entire history.

And there’s another distinction: meaning transmitted isn’t necessarily meaning received.

Silence can be agreement, defiance, affection, contempt, uncertainty, or simply silence. The receiver supplies context, sometimes correctly and sometimes not.

That’s why communication operates on several levels simultaneously: content, timing, tone, omission, expectation, and shared history.

Even nothing becomes information when something was expected.

And the reverse is equally interesting: sometimes saying everything is a way of revealing nothing. You can bury the important signal beneath so much noise that the listener never recognizes it.

A message’s power isn’t proportional to its length. It’s proportional to the meaning it successfully carries.

Which makes shared understanding rather extraordinary.

Two people can exchange a single look and communicate something that would take a stranger an hour to explain.

Or exchange a million words and never get on the same page.

And perhaps that’s the art of it: knowing when to elaborate, when to compress, and when to leave room for someone else to understand.

Sometimes, even an entire philosophy fits inside a wink. ;)